
# Original prompt:

I have a C++ project that I have not touched in more than 10 years.  I would like to port it to python and update it.  The current working directory, `/Users/westfall/Work/packages/dc3`, is largely empty but it should become the top-level directory of a git repository for the updated code.

The core of the code base is currently located at `/Users/westfallold/Work/scripts/dc3`, but it also uses "library" code located in other directories in `/Users/westfallold/Work/scripts`.  The scientific paper describing the code can be found at `/Users/westfall/Work/papers/dmIII/Westfall_etal_2011_ApJS_193_21.pdf`.  First, please read the paper for background on the purpose of the code base.

Second, provide a full report on the current state of the code.  Identify all dependencies and map the control flow of the various executables, including a description of how the code is compiled and used.  Where relevant, reference the Westfall et al. (2011) paper for context.  Write the report to `claude/dc3-original-implementation.md`.

Third, construct a phased development plan for rewriting and refactoring the code into a python package.  Use the pypeit package at `/Users/westfall/Work/packages/pypeit` as a template package structure.  The refactored package should leverage standard python libraries and, specifically, scientific python packages like numpy, scipy, matplotlib, astropy, specutils, etc.  Three specific items to note when developing this plan:

(1) The C++ code heavily leveraged code provided by the Numerical Recipes book.  Due to licensing issues, this prevented the code from being open source in the past.  It also meant that I used non-standard vector and matrix classes.  The refactor should heavily leverage existing python libraries, which I expect will greatly reduce the size of the code base and make it easier to maintain.  The code will be fully open source and be distributed with a 3-clause BSD license.

(2) The fitting approach used is generally slow.  Two specific avenues that I would like you to explore in terms of improving computation speed are (a) use of the FFTW library and (b) parallelization via multiprocessing.  As a template for the former, see the nirvana code base at `/Users/westfall/Work/packages/nirvana`.  Additionally, explore ways to incorporate automatic differentiation for model optimization using standard packages, like JAX.

(3) One enhancement I would like to make is that the code currently only allows a single template spectrum to be fit to each galaxy spectrum.  Please explore methods that will allow users to provide a full set of template that are then optimally mixed to provide the best fit to the galaxy spectrum.

It is also worth referencing the MaNGA Data Analysis Pipeline code at `/Users/westfall/Work/packages/mangadap`, which uses the `ppxf` package to fit galaxy spectra.  The refactor of `dc3` does not necessarily need to follow any of the specific patterns seen in that code base, but it is a useful reference for how it may be integrated into a larger workflow for fitting many galaxy spectra.

---

