# Response to `plan-reassessment` doc

## Items 1 - 3:

Treatment of resampling and resolution matching are subtle.

- The fitting code should never manipulate the flux distribution within the
  provided galaxy spectrum.  In terms of item 3, this contradicts our initial
  concept for de-redshifting the galaxy spectrum before beginning the fit
  iterations.  However, given the logarithmic sampling of the spectra in
  wavelength, we can recast this as an *approximate* de-reshifting (as you say,
  we'll be fitting the velocity offset anyway) using a simple pixel shift and
  truncation, not a *redistribution* of flux within the spectrum.  This shift
  and/or truncation can then be analytically propagated to the measured velocity
  offset.

- There will always be a difference in resolution between the template spectra
  and the galaxy spectrum (as described in W19).  Also, although it may be
  advantageous to use templates that have higher resolution than the galaxy
  spectra, it is not always possible.  Importantly, any resolution matching
  cannot involve a deconvolution because it leads to amplified noise.

- Because of the difference in computational expense between convolution by a
  wavelength-independent vs. wavelength-dependent sigma, the model evaluations
  internal to the fitting iterations prefer to use a wavelength-independent
  kernel.  This means the resolution matching step is a preparatory step before
  the fit is started.  But, acknowledging the previous two items, this
  preparatory step is not always possible, depending on the resolution of the
  template and galaxy spectra.  For item 2 specifically, this means I agree with
  the provided recommendation.  And, as I state below, the important thing is
  that whatever choice is made is correctly propagated through to the code used
  to determine the velocity dispersion corrections provided to the user.

- Preparation of the template spectra is a combination of both resolution
  matching *and* resampling (i.e., the pixel scale of the templates should match
  the galaxy data).  These two operations are not independent.  I.e., if the
  difference in resolution is large enough, resampling the template spectra to
  match the galaxy data will dramatically degrade its effective resolution
  (Nyquist sampling of the line-spread function).  This both limits the
  practical resolution offset allowed for the fit-prepared templates, and may
  motivate an oversampling of the template spectra that are then downgraded
  during the fitting process.  The latter is possible in `ppxf`, and DC3 could
  do the same in the construction of the B spectrum.

- The stellar velocity dispersion in galaxies is always non-zero.  This means
  there is always a little wiggle room that a user may be willing to take
  advantage of when trying to match the resolution of the template spectra to
  the galaxy spectra up to some pedestal offset.  If the pedestal must put the
  templates at a systematically lower resolution than the galaxy spectra, this
  imposes a floor on the measurable stellar velocity dispersion.  The difference
  between W11 and W19 is, I would say, largely an issue of framing.  In MaNGA,
  we had templates that were higher resolution than the galaxy spectra, so we
  could adopt a *positive* \delta\sigma_inst^2.  In the DiskMass survey, the
  template were observed with the same instrument as the galaxy data, but
  slightly different conditions in the system could lead to small resolution
  differences.  This meant that we tended to need to lower the resolution in the
  templates to keep a pedestal offset, such that we were effectively adopting a
  *negative* \delta\sigma_inst^2 (note the exponent).  In the DMS, this floor
  was seen as an observational limitation; i.e., a dispersion measurement below
  the floor meant that the dispersion was not measurable with the observational
  data.  This new version of DC3 should be able to track both cases, both
  positive and negative \delta\sigma_inst^2, but a warning needs to be issued
  when it is negative.  Perhaps we should adopt different nomenclature than
  \delta\sigma_inst^2, adopting something like a variance so we don't need to
  keep tracking the square and avoid the ambiguity of the imaginary number when
  the quantity is negative.

Having said all that, the proposed design in item 1 is generally my preferred
approach.  That is, the default should be to *try* to impose a constant offset
between the spectral resolution of the template and galaxy spectra, with the
template at higher resolution, if possible.  However, in general, I think users
will want to define the minimum sigma in the wavelength-dependent convolution
kernel instead of defining \delta directly; this is the approach taken for
setting up the instrumental dispersion of the emission-line templates in the
`mangadap` (see the `etpl_sinst*` objects in `mangdap.proc.sasuke.Sasuke.fit`).
With the significant caveat concerning how the pixelization changes the
effective resolution, users will generally want to make \delta as large as
possible, and they should not be expected to know the correct value off-hand.

Whatever choice is made here, the most important thing is ensuring that this
choice is propagated through the analysis that determines the
velocity-dispersion corrections.

In the limit where the velocity dispersion is "undetermined," it should be up to
the user whether or not they want to refit the spectra by fixing the *measured*
velocity dispersion to be 0.  In terms of hooks in the code, this means adding
the ability to fix the kinematic parameters to an input value and only fitting a
subset of the spectra provided.  At least for now, I don't think this means, e.g.,
adding an optional path that automatically preforms this refitting if the user
toggles a boolean input parameter.

