## Item 5:

None of the following are hard requirements.  We should be open to revisiting
them as we develop the code.

5.1: For now, we should leverage the templates in the `mangadap` repo.  Notably,
the templates used in the DiskMass survey are not distributed.  Some of the
templates are at `/Volumes/seshat/data/diskmass/mab_jul05_templates/Mg`;
however, I'm unsure of their provenance.  There is README that may be useful.

5.2: As alluded to in my response to items 1-3, the default value for \delta
should be the largest possible, but I don't think that is the parameter we
should expose to the user.  See my comments there.  However, to simplify your
last comment about \delta being spectrum dependent, we should expect \delta to
be the same for all spectra to be fit.  I.e., for each execution of the main
fitting script, we should only manipulate the resolution and sampling of the
template spectra once.

5.3: The outermost function should assume multiple spectra are provided, but it
should be able to operate on a single spectrum.  The innermost function ---
i.e., the function that is called to execute the fit after all the prep work has
been completed --- should operate on a single spectrum only.  The innermost
function, to my mind, represents the natural place for multiprocessing hooks.

5.4: As already stated in the plan doc, let's plan to pull phase 7 forward.  The
current doc notes that some aspects of phase 7 should be pulled forward to phase
4, but that others may remain in phase 7.  Please reassess.  And, yes, please
check W11 again for relevant measurement precision

5.5: Agreed that the current framing is likely wrong.  See my comments on items
1-3.

5.6:

    - The template(s) should always be resampled to the same wavelength grid as
      the galaxy spectra, up to an integer ratio in the number of samples.  The
      approach should mimic the `velscale_ratio` parameter in `ppxf`.  See my
      comments in the 4th bullet in my response to items 1-3.

    - Keep the path that doesn't require errors.  Ultimately, we may want to add
      a path that estimates the errors based on wavelength-dependent (moving
      window) measurements of the standard deviation in the model residuals.
      This should take the form of a post processing script; i.e., the workflow
      would be run the modeling without errors, run the script that estimates
      the errors, re-run the modeling with the estimated errors.  Leave this as
      a future development path.

    - I would suggest dropping the size estimate.  It's not a concern in whether
      or not I pursue the project.

    - Do not include test data in the `tests/` directory; i.e., there should be
      no `dc3/tests/data/` directory.

## Responses to "Follow-up Questions"

This responds to questions asked after the first revision of the
`plan-reassessment.md` doc, in its section 7.

### 7.1

Please read 

`/Users/westfallold/Work/literature/Cappellari_2017_MNRAS_466_798.pdf`

for useful context.  This is the paper where Michele first introduces the
analytic FFT of the Gaussian and Gauss-Hermite convolution kernels into `ppxf`.

Thanks for pointing out that I wasn't being clear on the "minimum sigma;" I
think pointing to the `Sasuke` class may have simply confused the issue.  To
directly answer your question, I was referring to (a); however, it is worth
being explicit about a few things.

The template preparation steps should be as follows (this does not necessarily
follow the algorithm currently used in the C++ DC3 code):

Step 1: Perform any requested resolution matching of the template spectra to the
galaxy spectra.

Step 2: Resample the (convolved) template spectra to match the sampling of the
templates, up to a user-defined oversampling (`velscale_ratio`).

Note that Step 1 will only ever be approximate.  A single resolution matching
operation will be applied to all templates once based on a *fiducial* measure of
the spectral resolution of the galaxy data.  Unless all galaxy spectra have
exactly the same spectral resolution (e.g., the spectra in each MaNGA
observation did not), this *fiducial* spectral resolution will not exactly match
any given galaxy spectrum that should be fit.  Importantly, Step 2 will *also*
alter the effective resolution of the templates; the latter is something
`mangadap` has never accounted for.  This means that, regardless of the
resolution matching approach, calculation of velocity-dispersion corrections
will always be an important step.

When the user requests resolution matching, we should plan to follow the
approach described by Appendix A in W19 (cf.
`mangadap.util.resolution.SpectralResolution.GaussianKernelDifference`) with
three caveats.  (1) A pedestal offset (option 2, W19 A) should be the default
approach, where the pedestal is determined by the user's choice of
\epsilon_\sigma.  (2) The code should
adjust the target resolution for the template spectrum to allow for a *negative*
`dvar_inst` to avoid or minimize the span of spectral regions that cannot meet the
provided target resolution.  (3) For regions of the template spectrum that
cannot meet the target spectral resolution, we should allow the user to choose
whether or not those regions should be masked.

All told, I think this means we need three parameters:

- `velscale_ratio`: the integer number of pixels in the prepared template
  spectra that map to a single pixel in the galaxy spectra (1 by default)

- `epsilon_sigma`: the minimum viable dispersion of the Gaussian convolution
  kernel.  Cappellari (2017) argues that, at least mathematically, the analytic
  FFT approach allows this to be 0. (0 by default)

- `sigma_floor`: the maximum floor allowed to accommodate spectral regions in
  the template spectra with lower resolution than the galaxy data.  This is used
  to set the minimum (negative) value of dvar_inst. (0 by default)

Log messages should warn the user when:

- the resampled template spectrum is not Nyquist sampled (`velscale_ratio`
  should be increased)

- spectral regions in the template cannot be matched to the target spectral
  resolution.

### 7.2

Yes, `ppxf` should stay a hard dependency for now.  A core difference between
`ppxf_util.varsmooth` and `mangadap`'s `convolution_variable_sigma` is that the
former uses the more robust analytic equation for the FFT of a Gaussian.  This
is the preferred approach.

### 7.3

Yes, let's stick with `dvar_inst`, and the sign convention matches my intent.

### 7.4

Your read is consistent with my intent, however, we will need to be clever about
how these constraints are passed to the program given the level of granularity
needed when large batches of spectra are passed to the outer loop.

### 7.5

Yes, replace with the Fourier phase shift.  It is a valuable departure from W11.

### 7.6

In my response to 7.1, I note the default `velscale_ratio` should be 1, but I
like the option of having the code determine a reasonable value automatically.
Despite the additional complexity, this will save users from having to determine
a good value and defer to DC3's approach.  A reasonable approach is to ensure
that the FWHM of the spectral LSF is sampled by at least 2 pixels.

### 7.7

We should follow the `PypeIt` pattern, but not via a separate development suite
repo.  PypeIt uses it's cache system to access test data that is located in
`pypeit/data/tests`.  The test data are not gitignored (the cache accesses them
via GitHub if they are not local), but that are not included in the package
release (i.e., the `pypeit/data/tests` directory is excluded by the
`MANIFEST.in` file).

## Responses to "New follow-up questions"

This responds to questions asked after the 2nd revision of the
`plan-reassessment.md` doc, in its section 8.

### 8.1

Yes, your proposed resolution was my intent.

### 8.2

(a) Yes, that is the intent here.  However, this is mostly a requirement for
appropriate use of the code and something we need to ensure is properly
documented.  That is, the code will assume that the instrumental dispersion
provided for both the template and galaxy data are determined for the
pre-pixelized data.  It will be up to the users to ensure they're providing the
correct resolution measurement.

(b) First, where does the \Delta^2/12 metric come from?  More generally, the correct path on this is currently unclear to me, particularly given the expectation from my response to (a) above.  I.e., I expect the resolution vectors to be provided pre-pixelized, and its unclear to me how the resampling propagates to this pre-pixelized measurement.  Nominally it should have no effect, but I expect there are practical limitations to this.  I expect we will need to characterize the effect empirically.

### 8.3

Let's hard-code the current 0.1 pixel limit but include a test at asserts this
is correct.  That should be sufficient to catch changes to `ppxf` by the
developers, but does not require a redetermination of the limit on every import.

### 8.4

I like your suggestion of a constraints table.  The table should be ascii so that is directly human readable and writable.  I prefer fixed-width columnated format to ecsv, but ecsv is preferred over FITS.
